FROM: Just like we need a base AMI when launching an EC2 instance, a Dockerfile needs a base image to start with. We specify the base image using the FROM instruction.
For example:
FROM ubuntu:22.04
This tells Docker to use the Ubuntu 22.04 image as the base image for building our custom image. We can then install the required applications, libraries, dependencies, and configuration on top of this base image.
Note: This is similar to customizing an AMI. In AWS, we can start with a base AMI and customize it with the software and configuration we need. Similarly, in Docker, we select a base image using FROM and then use instructions such as RUN, COPY, EXPOSE, and CMD to build our customized Docker image.


RUN: The base OS image may not contain all the libraries, packages, or applications required to run our application. We use the `RUN` instruction to execute commands during the image build process and install or configure everything our application needs.


COPY: We use the COPY instruction to copy our application source code or other required files from the build context into a specific location inside the Docker image.
For example:
COPY index.html /var/www/html/index.html
This copies our index.html file into Apache's web directory inside the image.


EXPOSE: We use the EXPOSE instruction to document the port on which the application inside the container is expected to listen.
For example:
EXPOSE 80
This indicates that the application is expected to listen on port 80 inside the container.


